course: dictionary
lesson: 2030
title: Confiscate: taking things away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes a word has a clue right next to it. In this story, the Rat had some invitations, and the text says he «confiscated the lot» (took them all away). The bracket gives the meaning, so confiscate means to take something away from someone.
explain: Teachers often confiscate things. A teacher may confiscate a phone, sweets or a toy during class, then give it back at the end of the day. The thing is taken because a rule says it is not allowed.
explain: In the law, confiscate can mean that police or the government officially take someone's property, for example because it was used in a crime. Ask yourself who is doing the taking and why. The reason tells you what kind of taking it is.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The Rat had read the invitations and took the lot away» still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Confiscate is a verb: confiscate, confiscates, confiscated, confiscating.
fact: «Confiscate» means to take something away from someone, usually as a punishment or because a rule says it is not allowed.
fact: In the law, «confiscate» means that police or the government officially take someone's property, for example because it was used in a crime.
fact: Common phrases are «confiscate a phone», «have something confiscated» and «confiscated by customs».
fact: The clue words in the story are the best way to check a new word: «(took them all away)» tells you what «confiscated» means.
exercise: The teacher said, «Your phone will be confiscated until Friday.» What does confiscated mean here? | Given back with a smile | Charged up for the lesson | *Taken away until Friday | Hidden under a book
exercise: Customs officers at the airport confiscated the stolen paintings. What happened to the paintings? | Sold at a market | Hung up in a gallery | *Officially taken away from the people carrying them | Lent to a friend
exercise: Which word means the same as confiscate? | Return | Lend | Hide | *Take away
test: Mum says, «I'm going to confiscate that toy if you keep throwing it.» What will Mum do? | *Take the toy away | Smile and say well done | Buy a new toy | Throw it in the bin
test: «The Rat had read the invitations and confiscated the lot.» What did the Rat do with the invitations? | Posted them to his friends | *Took all of them away | Read them out loud | Ate them all
exam: Why might a police officer confiscate something? | Because someone asked to borrow it | Because it is a lovely present | *Because it was used in a crime | Because it is nice to look at
